  "caption": {
    "text": "OpenAI CEO Sam Altman said in an interview on Politico's Decoded that the world should accept \"some bad things happening\" for the benefits of AI, and the remark drew sharp criticism.\n\nAltman named hacks, scams and other bad things as costs, saying people will do far more good than bad with AI. He framed willingness to accept harm as a key difference between OpenAI and Anthropic, and said OpenAI's lighter-touch approach to regulation comes with accepting that some bad things will happen. He also said stronger regulation is still needed, and that too much power held by one lab, a few companies or one country is another way AI can go wrong. Lighter-touch regulation is his term for some rules, but less restrictive than the stricter approach he attributes to Anthropic.\n\nPatrick Gaspard of the Center for American Progress called the remark \"freaking insane\". No OpenAI response to the criticism was found.\n\nWould you accept some AI harm in exchange for its benefits?\n\nFollow Helios Group to see whether OpenAI answers the criticism.\n\nSource: The Verge, Common Dreams and SiliconANGLE, October 5, 2026.",
